      "name": "DispatchCallbackByIndex",
      "signature": "int DispatchCallbackByIndex(byte * pCallbackIndex, int nCount)",
      "calling_convention": "__fastcall",
      "return_type": "undefined4",
      "comment": "Dispatches callback functions from a table based on an index value.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize return value to zero (EAX = 0)\n2. Check if nCount is zero - return 0 immediately if true (JA check)\n3. Load the index byte from pCallbackIndex pointer (MOV BL, [ECX])\n4. Compare index against 0x1A (26) to validate range - return 0 if invalid\n5. Look up callback function pointer in table at DAT_6fa2a968 using index as offset (ESI = [ESI*4 + 0x6fa2a968])\n6. Test if callback pointer is NULL - skip invocation if null\n7. Call the callback function pointer (CALL ESI)\n8. Return with RET 0x4 (fastcall cleanup: 2 parameters * 4 bytes each)\n\nParameters:\npCallbackIndex (byte *): Pointer to callback index byte (range 0x00-0x19, validated)\nnCount (int): Count or validity flag (must be non-zero to proceed)\n\nReturns:\nReturn value from callback function if invoked, or 0 if:\n- nCount is zero\n- Index is >= 0x1A\n- Callback pointer is NULL\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Index range: 0x00 to 0x19 (26 possible callbacks)\n- Callback table: DAT_6fa2a968 contains function pointers (4 bytes each)\n- NULL callbacks: Safely skipped without calling\n- Return cleanup: RET 0x4 indicates __fastcall convention with 2 parameters",
